===Glyph origin===
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|生|t1=growth of plants|丹|t2=[[cinnabar]]|ls=ic}}. Cinnabar was used for dyeing, and by extension, came to imply “color” in general, giving the combined meaning “color of growing plants” → “blue-green”.

In the modern glyph, the top component is reduced to {{zh-l|*龶}}, and the bottom component resembles the unrelated {{zh-l|*月|moon}}.

The {{w|Second round of simplified Chinese characters|second-round simplified form}} of the character is based on the calligraphic form of the character.

===Etymology 1===
{{zh-forms|t2=靑|ss=𰀈|alt=寈,㞭,𡗡,𡴏,𡴑,𡷉,𤯝,𤯞}}
From {{inh-lite|zh|sit-pro|*s-riŋ ~ s-r(j)aŋ||to live; to be alive; to give birth; raw; green}}. Cognate with {{och-l|生|to live}}, {{och-l|蒼|green; blue|id=1}}, {{och-l|性|nature}}, {{och-l|姓|surname}}. Note {{och-l|蒼|id=1}} may be an old dialect variant of {{och-l|青}} ({{zh-ref|Schuessler, 2007}}).

